<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip IV of Macedon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 297 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a> and succeeded him as king of Macedon in 297 BCE. He reigned only a few months before dying of illness. His younger brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> II and Alexander V then quarrelled over the throne, allowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrius-i-of-macedon">Demetrius Poliorcetes</a> to seize Macedon in 294 BCE and end the Antipatrid dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-macedon.jpg" alt="Philip IV of Macedon" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yale University · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philip_IV_of_Macedon_copper_coin;_struck_c._297%E2%80%93296_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
